From: Keenetic SDK Builder <developers@keenetic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fix: never call cond_resched() while the SIMD context is held

The encrypt/decrypt workers take the SIMD context once and keep it for the
whole queue drain, then call cond_resched() inside that region. On arm64
that means the worker can go to sleep with kernel_neon_busy still set on
its CPU.

The region looks safe because kernel_neon_begin() ends with
preempt_disable(), and simd_relax() is supposed to release NEON before a
reschedule. Neither holds on a 4.9-ndm target:

  - CONFIG_PREEMPT_COUNT is not set, so preempt_disable() compiles to a
    bare barrier() (include/linux/preempt.h). The task is fully
    schedulable inside the NEON region.
  - simd_relax() is wrapped in #ifdef CONFIG_PREEMPT (compat/simd), and
    the kernel is CONFIG_PREEMPT_NONE, so it compiles to nothing.
  - cond_resched(), on the other hand, *is* compiled (it is built for
    !CONFIG_PREEMPT) and should_resched(0) is true, because preempt_count()
    really is 0 here. It schedules.

Once the worker sleeps with kernel_neon_busy set, the next user of NEON on
that CPU dies. The reported path is IPsec ESP: ablk_encrypt() sees
may_use_simd() == false and defers the request to cryptd, and the cryptd
worker then calls ctr_encrypt(), which begins NEON unconditionally:

  Process kworker/1:1
  kernel_neon_begin+0xa8/0xc0      <- BUG_ON(!may_use_simd())
  ctr_encrypt+0x5c/0x180
  cryptd_blkcipher_crypt+0x48/0x100
  cryptd_blkcipher_encrypt+0x1c/0x40
  cryptd_queue_worker+0x78/0xc0
  process_one_work+0x188/0x340

Reproduced on two NC-1812 (mt7988, 4.9-ndm-5) carrying an ESP tunnel next
to a busy AmneziaWG interface: a reboot every 5-6 hours on both. A build
with NEON disabled in the module ran 49 hours on both with no crash, which
is what put the module's own NEON region under suspicion.

The stock keenetic wireguard.ko (1.0.20200623) holds NEON exactly as long,
but has no cond_resched() inside the region, which is why it has never
shown this. The call was inherited from the in-tree driver, where the
crypto library begins and ends NEON per chunk and there is no region to
break.

Release the context around the reschedule instead of relying on
simd_relax(), so sleeping with NEON held becomes impossible regardless of
the kernel's preemption model. This also drops the now redundant
simd_relax() call: on CONFIG_PREEMPT kernels the new block does exactly
what simd_relax() did.

Same bug is present upstream as of v3.0.20260805.

Signed-off-by: Keenetic SDK Builder <developers@keenetic.com>
---
--- a/src/send.c
+++ b/src/send.c
@@ -438,11 +438,15 @@
 		}
 		wg_queue_enqueue_per_peer_tx(first, state);
 
-#ifdef COMPAT_CRYPTO_IS_ZINC
-		simd_relax(&simd_context);
-#endif
-		if (need_resched())
-			cond_resched();
+		if (need_resched()) {
+#ifdef COMPAT_CRYPTO_IS_ZINC
+			simd_put(&simd_context);
+#endif
+			cond_resched();
+#ifdef COMPAT_CRYPTO_IS_ZINC
+			simd_get(&simd_context);
+#endif
+		}
 	}
 #ifdef COMPAT_CRYPTO_IS_ZINC
 	simd_put(&simd_context);
--- a/src/receive.c
+++ b/src/receive.c
@@ -610,11 +610,15 @@
 					      COMPAT_MAYBE_SIMD_CONTEXT(&simd_context))) ?
 				PACKET_STATE_CRYPTED : PACKET_STATE_DEAD;
 		wg_queue_enqueue_per_peer_rx(skb, state);
-#ifdef COMPAT_CRYPTO_IS_ZINC
-		simd_relax(&simd_context);
-#endif
-		if (need_resched())
-			cond_resched();
+		if (need_resched()) {
+#ifdef COMPAT_CRYPTO_IS_ZINC
+			simd_put(&simd_context);
+#endif
+			cond_resched();
+#ifdef COMPAT_CRYPTO_IS_ZINC
+			simd_get(&simd_context);
+#endif
+		}
 	}
 
 #ifdef COMPAT_CRYPTO_IS_ZINC
